1
00:00:01,080 --> 00:00:04,908
In this video, we'll explore how to
advocate for and create continuous

2
00:00:04,908 --> 00:00:09,060
improvements in a project by
learning about process improvements.

3
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:13,830
Continuous improvement is an ongoing
effort to improve products or services.

4
00:00:13,830 --> 00:00:18,185
It helps ensure that a product steadily
makes its way toward the best possible

5
00:00:18,185 --> 00:00:19,720
outcome.

6
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:23,475
Continuous improvement begins with
recognizing when processes and

7
00:00:23,475 --> 00:00:27,350
tasks need to be created,
eliminated, or improved.

8
00:00:27,350 --> 00:00:31,736
Then a project manager must plan for and
implement changes to keep the project on

9
00:00:31,736 --> 00:00:35,090
track. That's where process
improvement comes from.

10
00:00:35,090 --> 00:00:38,698
Process improvement is the practice
of identifying, analyzing,

11
00:00:38,698 --> 00:00:42,813
and improving existing processes to
enhance the performance of your team and

12
00:00:42,813 --> 00:00:44,346
to develop best practices

13
00:00:44,346 --> 00:00:47,178
or to optimize consumer experiences.

14
00:00:47,178 --> 00:00:49,760
When working with your
process improvements,

15
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:52,601
using a controlled environment
in an experiment can

16
00:00:52,601 --> 00:00:57,220
help you understand if the changes
you're considering fix your problem.

17
00:00:57,220 --> 00:00:58,843
A control is an experiment or

18
00:00:58,843 --> 00:01:02,900
observation designed to minimize
the effects of variables.

19
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:06,772
Control groups are representative
samples that help you to determine that

20
00:01:06,772 --> 00:01:09,599
the differences between your
experimental groups and

21
00:01:09,599 --> 00:01:12,740
the norm are due to your changes,
rather than something else.

22
00:01:13,741 --> 00:01:16,800
They eliminate alternative
explanations for your results.

23
00:01:17,801 --> 00:01:22,240
If you aren't familiar with this, that's
okay, because I'll break it down for you.

24
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:27,910
For example, you observe a problem with
your process and put forward a hypothesis,

25
00:01:27,910 --> 00:01:32,750
which is an educated guess about what's
causing the problem and how you'd fix it.

26
00:01:32,750 --> 00:01:35,402
Then you change one
variable in the system,

27
00:01:35,402 --> 00:01:39,640
keeping the control group the same,
and observe your results again.

28
00:01:41,450 --> 00:01:44,850
Let's put this in the context
of Project Plant Pal scenario.

29
00:01:44,850 --> 00:01:47,581
Business is booming for
Office Green, and demand for

30
00:01:47,581 --> 00:01:51,280
your team's new Plant Pal
service is rapidly growing.

31
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:55,409
To meet the demand, suppliers have
streamlined their process of packing boxes

32
00:01:55,409 --> 00:01:58,690
and putting all plants into
a one-size-fits-all box.

33
00:01:58,690 --> 00:02:02,969
Let's say you're using just one box
size to ship all of your plants.

34
00:02:02,969 --> 00:02:07,954
For smaller plants, there's more padding
added to the box to fill the extra space,

35
00:02:07,954 --> 00:02:11,160
and the plants usually arrive intact.

36
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:15,832
But larger plants have to be squeezed into
the boxes and sometimes arrive damaged,

37
00:02:15,832 --> 00:02:18,020
according to customer surveys.

38
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:23,160
To fix this problem, you hypothesize
a potential solution by posing a question.

39
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:26,848
Would more of the large plants have
arrived intact if they were shipped in

40
00:02:26,848 --> 00:02:30,118
bigger boxes using the same padding
we use for the small plants?

41
00:02:30,118 --> 00:02:32,450
Here is where your control comes in.

42
00:02:32,450 --> 00:02:36,280
You continue to ship half of the larger
plants in the original boxes.

43
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:37,909
This is your control group.

44
00:02:37,909 --> 00:02:42,290
And you experiment by shipping
the other half in bigger boxes.

45
00:02:42,290 --> 00:02:45,730
Nothing else is different about
the boxes except the size.

46
00:02:45,730 --> 00:02:49,146
The shape, thickness,
box supplier, delivery
addresses—absolutely

47
00:02:49,147 --> 00:02:52,730
everything else
stays exactly the same.

48
00:02:52,730 --> 00:02:56,590
After the larger plants are delivered,
you conduct a new survey.

49
00:02:56,590 --> 00:03:00,770
If more larger plants arrived intact,
your hypothesis is confirmed.

50
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:04,762
If the results are the same as
they were before the experiment,

51
00:03:04,762 --> 00:03:09,510
you'll need to try something else to
solve the problem of your damaged plants.

52
00:03:09,510 --> 00:03:13,059
Working in a controlled environment
isn't the only way to ensure continuous

53
00:03:13,059 --> 00:03:14,540
improvements.

54
00:03:14,540 --> 00:03:19,700
There are various data-driven improvement
frameworks, like DMAIC and PDCA.

55
00:03:21,530 --> 00:03:25,640
I'll define those and present them in the
context of Plant Pals in the next video.

56
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:26,200
See you there.
